\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces GMOR proper on trained checkpoints, with a linear ambient spurion. (a) The collapse $\mu ^2F^2=\delta \Sigma $ over $8$ decades of $\delta $, $8$ trained $SO(2)$ checkpoints. (b) Absolute deviation pinned at the $f64$ floor ($\le 1.33{\times }10^{-15}$) against the $\epsilon /\delta $ relative-roundoff line of the autodiff Hessian --- the small-$\delta $ relative residual is a probe artifact, not a law violation. (c) The running condensate $\Sigma (\delta )/\Sigma (0)$ under the linear spurion against the angular tilt's exactly flat $\Sigma \equiv r^*$ --- the shipped tilt cannot see the condensate. (d) The low-energy-constant ratio tending to $1$ as the chiral expansion parameter $x\to 0$, with slope $-1.05$. \emph  {Designed testbed, analytic spurion, trained checkpoints, therefore verification.}}}{17}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gmor}{{5}{17}{GMOR proper on trained checkpoints, with a linear ambient spurion. (a) The collapse $\mu ^2F^2=\delta \Sigma $ over $8$ decades of $\delta $, $8$ trained $SO(2)$ checkpoints. (b) Absolute deviation pinned at the $f64$ floor ($\le 1.33{\times }10^{-15}$) against the $\epsilon /\delta $ relative-roundoff line of the autodiff Hessian --- the small-$\delta $ relative residual is a probe artifact, not a law violation. (c) The running condensate $\Sigma (\delta )/\Sigma (0)$ under the linear spurion against the angular tilt's exactly flat $\Sigma \equiv r^*$ --- the shipped tilt cannot see the condensate. (d) The low-energy-constant ratio tending to $1$ as the chiral expansion parameter $x\to 0$, with slope $-1.05$. \emph {Designed testbed, analytic spurion, trained checkpoints, therefore verification.}}{figure.5}{}}
